{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"deque-list","title":"deque, list & forward_list","group":"STL Containers & Iterators","order":50,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>std::deque</code> is a double-ended queue with O(1) push/pop at both ends and random access; <code>std::list</code> is a doubly linked list with O(1) splicing anywhere.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left-right\"></i><code>std::deque</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;deque&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::deque&lt;int&gt; d{3, 4};\n    d.push_front(2);\n    d.push_front(1);\n    d.push_back(5);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; d.front() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; d.back() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; d[2] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; d.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    d.pop_front();\n    d.pop_back();\n    for (int x : d) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 5 3 5\n234</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Internally a deque is an array of fixed-size blocks, so growing at either end never moves existing elements (references stay valid on push at the ends) — but elements aren't contiguous overall.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i><code>std::list</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;iterator&gt;\n#include &lt;list&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::list&lt;int&gt; l{5, 1, 4, 1, 3};\n    auto it = std::next(l.begin(), 2);     // no random access: walk there\n    l.insert(it, 99);                      // O(1) once you have the iterator\n    l.remove(1);                           // remove all 1s\n    l.sort();                              // member sort (std::sort needs random access)\n    for (int x : l) std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::list&lt;int&gt; other{7, 8};\n    l.splice(l.begin(), other);            // move nodes from other in O(1) — no copies\n    l.unique();                            // drop consecutive duplicates\n    l.reverse();\n    for (int x : l) std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"| other size \" &lt;&lt; other.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 4 5 99\n99 5 4 3 8 7 | other size 0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i><code>std::forward_list</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;forward_list&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::forward_list&lt;int&gt; fl{2, 3};       // singly linked: one pointer per node, no size()\n    fl.push_front(1);\n    fl.insert_after(fl.begin(), 10);       // only insert *after* a position\n    for (int x : fl) std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 10 2 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>vector</code></th><th><code>deque</code></th><th><code>list</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Random access <code>[i]</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>push/pop front</td><td>O(n)</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Insert in middle</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong> with iterator</td></tr><tr><td>Contiguous memory</td><td>Yes</td><td>No (chunks)</td><td>No (nodes)</td></tr><tr><td>Iterators stay valid on insert</td><td>No (reallocation)</td><td>No (but refs do at ends)</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Memory overhead</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium</td><td>2 pointers per element</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you actually use std::list?</summary><div><p>When you need iterators/references to stay valid while inserting and erasing elsewhere, or you splice elements between lists in O(1) — e.g. an LRU cache (list + hash map of iterators). For plain sequences, <code>vector</code> wins on speed and memory.</p></div></details></section>"}